### api/auth/oauth — sign in with Google or GitHub
#
# The provider leg cannot be scripted: /start's 302 goes to a real Google or
# GitHub consent screen, and completing it needs a human. What follows are the
# requests that do not need the provider, plus the manual steps for the two
# that do, as comments -- the same idiom common.http uses for concurrency
# checks no interactive client can produce.
#
# Requires OAUTH_ENABLED=true and real GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID/SECRET or
# GITHUB_CLIENT_ID/SECRET in the running stack's environment. Against the
# local docker-compose stack, which sets none of these by default, the whole
# provider round trip is covered instead by
# src/api/auth/e2e/oauth-signin.int.test.ts and oauth-linking.int.test.ts
# against a local stub, and the refusal paths below still exercise fully
# without any provider at all.
#
# Variables come from .env in this folder. See README.md.

@baseUrl = {{$dotenv baseUrl}}
@email = {{$dotenv email}}
@password = {{$dotenv password}}
@cookieName = {{$dotenv cookieName}}
@oauthCookieName = {{$dotenv oauthCookieName}}
@forgedOauthCookie = {{$dotenv forgedOauthCookie}}

###
# @name startGoogle
# 302 to accounts.google.com, carrying client_id, redirect_uri, an S256 PKCE
# challenge, state and nonce. Sets the state cookie ({{oauthCookieName}}).
#
# Manual step: follow the redirect, sign in, and the provider redirects back
# to redirect_uri with ?code=...&state=.... That completes the flow -- there
# is no way to script picking a Google account.
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/oauth/google/start

###
# @name startGitHub
# 302 to github.com/login/oauth/authorize. Same manual step as above.
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/oauth/github/start

###
# @name startUnknownProvider
# 404 NOT_FOUND. An unknown provider name is an unknown route, not a
# validation error about a route that might exist -- and answers identically
# to a real provider name with no configured credentials.
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/oauth/bitbucket/start

###
# @name callbackNoCookie
# 302 to {WEB_BASE_URL}/login?error=OAUTH_STATE_INVALID. REST Client sends the
# jar's cookie automatically, so this blanks it explicitly.
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/oauth/google/callback?code=whatever&state=whatever
Cookie:

###
# @name callbackMismatchedState
# 302 to .../login?error=OAUTH_STATE_INVALID. A well-formed state cookie value
# that does not match any pending row -- the shape a forged or stale cookie
# takes.
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/oauth/google/callback?code=whatever&state=whatever
Cookie: {{oauthCookieName}}={{forgedOauthCookie}}

###
# @name callbackStaleState
# Run startGoogle above, do not follow the redirect, then run this within ten
# minutes of that request but with a state that does not match the one it
# issued: 302 to .../login?error=OAUTH_STATE_INVALID. The genuinely expired
# case (wait ten minutes, or shorten OAUTH_STATE_TTL_MS) answers the same way.
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/oauth/google/callback?code=whatever&state=not-the-issued-state

###
# @name login
# Needed for the authenticated requests below.
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "{{email}}",
  "password": "{{password}}"
}

###
# @name linkGoogle
# 200 {"redirectUrl": "..."}. Sets the state cookie the same way /start does;
# unlike /start this requires a session. Follow redirectUrl manually to
# complete the same provider leg as startGoogle.
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/oauth/google/link

###
# @name linkNoSession
# 401 UNAUTHENTICATED.
POST {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/oauth/google/link
Cookie:

###
# @name identities
# 200 [{"provider":"google","email":"...","linkedAt":"..."}], or [] before
# linking anything.
GET {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/identities

###
# @name unlinkGoogle
# 204 if a password or another identity remains; 409 LAST_CREDENTIAL if this
# is the account's only way to sign in.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/oauth/google

###
# @name unlinkNeverLinked
# 404 NOT_FOUND.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/oauth/github

###
# @name unlinkNoSession
# 401 UNAUTHENTICATED.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/v1/auth/oauth/google
Cookie:

# Not here: the rate limit on /start and /callback (auth_attempts, ip scope
# only -- see common.http for the same idiom), and the last-credential
# concurrency guarantee (two unlinks fired at once). Both need many requests
# arriving together, which no interactive client produces; both are covered by
# src/api/auth/e2e/oauth-signin.int.test.ts and
# src/api/auth/repositories/oauth-identity.repository.int.test.ts.
